-- Course: Bachelor of Music (Honours) (115736D)
UPDATE courses SET
    course_description = '<p>Adelaide University’s Bachelor of Music (Honours) will challenge you, sharpening your musical skills and research capabilities. Research an area of music you’re passionate about. Learn to craft music that stands out. </p><p>With a major in Creative Practice, you’ll gain a competitive edge in the job market with your specialised skills and knowledge. Create and perform original compositions. Conduct and present research in areas relevant to composition, song writing, recording, production and sonic arts. Graduate with a diverse skillset, preparing you for a variety of roles. You might pursue a career as a musician or progress into music research. </p>',
    course_duration_per_week = 208,
    total_course_duration = '4 year(s) full-time',
    offshore_tuition_fee = 45600,
    onshore_tuition_fee = NULL,
    entry_requirements = '<table><tbody><tr><td><strong>Academic Requirements</strong></td><td><ul><li>Completion of a secondary education qualification equivalent to the South Australian Certificate of Education (SACE).</li><li>Completion of an award from a registered training organisation (RTO) at Certificate IV (AQF level 4) or higher.</li><li>Successful completion of at least 6 months full-time study (or equivalent part-time) of an undergraduate diploma (AQF level 5) or higher qualification at a recognised higher education institution.</li><li>In cases where there are more eligible applicants than available places, admission will be competitive with ranks based on the entry criteria.</li></ul></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Interview</strong></td><td><ul><li>All applicants are required to register for an audition for each major and/or instrument they are applying for (e.g. voice and strings requires two auditions).</li><li>Selection will be based on audition score as long as academic thresholds are met.</li><li>Auditions take place in November each year.</li><li>If interstate or overseas and cannot attend an in-person audition, can opt to submit a recorded audition when completing registration form.</li><li>Register: complete Honours Music Programs registration audition form.</li><li>Register: pay non-refundable audition registration fee online via online portal.</li><li>Register: read audition requirements and prepare material.</li><li>Contact auditions@adelaide.edu.au for fee/payment help.</li></ul></td></tr><tr><td><strong>English Proficiency</strong></td><td><ul><li>International students who speak English as an additional language must have obtained one of the following standards within the last two years prior to admission.</li><li>IELTS Overall 6.5, Reading 6, Listening 6, Speaking 6, Writing 6.</li><li>Where previous study/work experience was conducted in English, application must be accompanied by certified documentation certifying language of instruction/employment was English.</li><li>Possession of qualifications in addition to academic entry requirements does not guarantee a place; applications considered individually and selection is competitive.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table>',
    apply_form = 'https://adelaideuni.edu.au/study/degrees/bachelor-of-music-honours-creative-practice/',
    updated_at = NOW()
WHERE cricos_course_code = '115736D';

-- Course: Bachelor of Construction Management (Honours) (115688G)
UPDATE courses SET
    course_description = '<p>The modern construction industry is booming. It requires adaptable leaders who can navigate complex construction techniques and manage budgets and teams – especially for large-scale projects. That’s where you come in.</p><p>With our Bachelor of Construction Management (Honours), you’ll become a driving force in this dynamic sector, overseeing projects from inception to completion. You’ll ensure projects stay on-time and on-budget, meeting both finance and quality benchmarks.</p><p>Adelaide University’s program is forward-thinking and shaped by industry insights. It has a focus on digital technology and sustainability, aligning it with current trends and industry requirements. With 150 hours of work placements built into the degree, you’ll gain real-world skills alongside theoretical knowledge.</p><p>Develop a comprehensive insight into the entire project lifecycle. Explore modern construction materials and project management techniques. Gain an understanding of local building codes and building regulations. With your technical and practical skills, you’ll be ready for a wide range of management roles in large-scale commercial, infrastructure and residential projects.</p>',
